WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:08.110
Your build file asks for a library, commons-lang3, at version 3.17.0.

2
00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:10.213
You wrote it down yourself.

3
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.877
So which version does your app run with?

4
00:00:12.877 --> 00:00:14.425
Surely the one you wrote.

5
00:00:14.425 --> 00:00:15.540
Hold that thought.

6
00:00:16.333 --> 00:00:17.636
Picture our bakery.

7
00:00:17.636 --> 00:00:20.996
To save time, it buys ready-made croissant dough.

8
00:00:20.996 --> 00:00:22.711
Here's its shopping list.

9
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:25.452
One morning a dairy announces a recall.

10
00:00:25.452 --> 00:00:27.573
A batch of its butter is unsafe.

11
00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:30.663
The head baker checks the list, and relaxes.

12
00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:31.928
Butter isn't on it.

13
00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:34.922
Then someone reads the small print on the dough.

14
00:00:34.922 --> 00:00:37.243
Made with butter, from that very dairy.

15
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:38.609
The list was honest.

16
00:00:38.609 --> 00:00:43.387
It just showed only what the bakery bought, and nothing about what came inside.

17
00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:46.615
Software has the same blind spot.

18
00:00:46.615 --> 00:00:49.533
Our lab app, hello-jvm, has two projects.

19
00:00:49.533 --> 00:00:52.521
app uses greeting, our own little library.

20
00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:56.245
greeting uses a library from outside, commons-text.

21
00:00:56.245 --> 00:00:57.437
That's the dough.

22
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:04.128
Every published library comes with a small metadata file that lists its own dependencies.

23
00:01:04.128 --> 00:01:05.302
The small print.

24
00:01:05.302 --> 00:01:08.969
commons-text's says: I need commons-lang3, 3.18.0.

25
00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:11.293
That's the butter inside the dough.

26
00:01:11.293 --> 00:01:14.932
A library you get because a library you asked for needs it.

27
00:01:14.932 --> 00:01:17.153
It's called a transitive dependency.

28
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:21.688
And app itself asks for commons-lang3 3.17.0 directly.

29
00:01:21.688 --> 00:01:23.704
The line we started with.

30
00:01:23.867 --> 00:01:28.204
Two paths, two versions, and the kitchen stocks only one jar.

31
00:01:28.204 --> 00:01:29.341
Pause and guess.

32
00:01:29.341 --> 00:01:30.692
Which version runs?

33
00:01:32.867 --> 00:01:33.384
3.18.0.

34
00:01:33.384 --> 00:01:36.490
Your own declaration gets no extra weight.

35
00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:41.740
When versions clash, Gradle's rule is simple: the highest version wins.

36
00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:48.639
The dependencies report shows it with an arrow: asked for 3.17.0, got 3.18.0.

37
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:59.239
The bet is that the library asking for the newer version may need something only that version has, while code written for the older one usually runs on the newer too.

38
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.989
If you know Maven, the other build tool, this surprises you.

39
00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:05.920
There, the declaration nearest your project wins.

40
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:07.766
So far so good.

41
00:02:07.766 --> 00:02:12.723
Now let's look somewhere else: not where the app runs, but where it compiles.

42
00:02:12.867 --> 00:02:16.145
A classpath is the list of jars a program can see.

43
00:02:16.145 --> 00:02:18.768
app has one for running, with all three.

44
00:02:18.933 --> 00:02:21.715
And it has a separate one for compiling.

45
00:02:21.715 --> 00:02:22.619
What's on it?

46
00:02:22.619 --> 00:02:23.732
Pause and guess.

47
00:02:25.867 --> 00:02:26.960
Two surprises.

48
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.834
commons-text is missing.

49
00:02:28.834 --> 00:02:35.551
And commons-lang3 is still 3.17.0 here, because each classpath is resolved on its own.

50
00:02:35.733 --> 00:02:41.372
commons-text is missing because of one word in greeting's build file: implementation.

51
00:02:41.533 --> 00:02:43.154
Think of a sauce you sell.

52
00:02:43.154 --> 00:02:47.204
The stock inside is your business, and customers never handle it.

53
00:02:47.204 --> 00:02:53.436
That's implementation: needed to compile and run greeting, but hidden from the projects that use it.

54
00:02:53.600 --> 00:03:00.020
So if app tries to import a class from commons-text, the compiler refuses: unresolved reference.

55
00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:03.832
The stock inside someone else's sauce isn't yours to use.

56
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.570
Now say your label reads: serve on Brand X crackers.

57
00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:12.443
The crackers are part of what you promise, and customers need them too.

58
00:03:12.443 --> 00:03:13.198
That's api.

59
00:03:13.198 --> 00:03:18.896
It also puts the library on the compile classpath of every project that uses yours.

60
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.983
Three habits come out of this picture.

61
00:03:22.133 --> 00:03:22.417
One.

62
00:03:22.417 --> 00:03:24.261
Default to implementation.

63
00:03:24.261 --> 00:03:29.651
Use api only when a type from that library appears in your public functions.

64
00:03:29.651 --> 00:03:35.963
It keeps insides private, and an upgrade doesn't recompile every project that uses yours.

65
00:03:36.133 --> 00:03:36.389
Two.

66
00:03:36.389 --> 00:03:42.664
When a scanner reports a vulnerable version that no build file mentions, that's the butter recall.

67
00:03:42.664 --> 00:03:45.737
dependencyInsight shows which path brings it in.

68
00:03:45.737 --> 00:03:50.923
Then declare the fixed version yourself, and highest wins lifts every path to it.

69
00:03:51.067 --> 00:03:51.502
Three.

70
00:03:51.502 --> 00:03:56.368
Compiling against 3.17.0 and running on 3.18.0 is usually harmless.

71
00:03:56.368 --> 00:04:03.921
But if a method you call changed, the code compiles fine, then fails at run time with NoSuchMethodError.

72
00:04:03.921 --> 00:04:09.077
The lesson's deeper section shows how to make the two classpaths agree.

73
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:10.879
So, the moral.

74
00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:14.644
Your build file shows what you bought, not what came inside.

75
00:04:14.644 --> 00:04:16.966
When versions meet, the highest wins.

76
00:04:16.966 --> 00:04:20.480
And implementation keeps your ingredients your business.

77
00:04:20.667 --> 00:04:22.927
The version you wrote at the start?

78
00:04:22.927 --> 00:04:24.541
A request, not a promise.

79
00:04:24.733 --> 00:04:27.446
Quick check, with made up numbers.

80
00:04:27.446 --> 00:04:30.478
app asks for version 2.1 of a library.

81
00:04:30.478 --> 00:04:33.429
Another library it uses asks for 2.3.

82
00:04:33.429 --> 00:04:34.626
Which one runs?

83
00:04:34.626 --> 00:04:35.903
Pause and think.

84
00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:38.376
2.3.

85
00:04:38.376 --> 00:04:42.475
The highest version wins, no matter who asked for it.
